<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John of Austria the Younger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1629–1679</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Juan José de Austria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">John of Austria</a> the Younger was the illegitimate son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-spain">Philip IV of Spain</a> and an actress, recognised by his father and given high commands. In 1648 he recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> from the revolutionary republic set up after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masaniello">Masaniello</a>'s revolt, and he later fought in Catalonia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and Portugal. In 1677 he seized power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and governed as chief minister of Charles II until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-of-austria-the-younger.jpg" alt="John of Austria the Younger" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan Carreño de Miranda · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juanjosedeaustria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
